title two pier trees, two refusals, two right answers -- hush discards its copies, dream rebases its own laps
prompt Keaton pasted both pier refusals: hush's fast-forward refused over three dirty files, and dream's refused as diverged.
think Same family, opposite cures, and telling them apart is the whole counsel. Hush's tree was born by cp -r from the pier's ~/grain, so its dirty ITINERARY, REDS, and day-shelf index are COPIES of the source's in-flight state whose originals still stand in ~/grain-dream -- the discard loses only duplicates: rm the copied loop state, stash clear (copies too), git reset --hard xy/main onto 83783bea99, arm. Dream's tree IS the renamed original, holding real unpushed signed lap commits beneath the same dirty leavings -- nothing there is a duplicate, so nothing is discarded: stash the leavings under a named message (the round-open's own park pattern), pull --rebase onto the moved xy, push both remotes so the laps come home, arm. The conflict fallback follows the seat prompt's own law -- abort, park the package on a pier/dream branch, reset to xy/main, and the parked commits wait for review.
obs The diverged state also explains itself: Dream's loop landed laps after 52c49cecb that never pushed before its loop ended, while Sound pushed three rounds from the Mac. The rebase brings both stories into one line without a force anywhere.
